  "text": "umably, the smallest possible area of $\\triangle PQR$ would be related to the radius of its circumcircle. If the diameter of the circumcircle of $\\triangle PSR$ is 14 cm, the radius is $14/2 = 7$ cm.\n\nThe circumcentre of $\\triangle PQR$ is the same as the circumcentre of $\\triangle PSR$. The circumradius of $\\triangle PQR$ is also 7 cm. However, the problem asks for the smallest possible area of $\\triangle PQR$, and since the triangle is equilateral $(PR = PQ)$, we need to find a related value for the area.\n\nThe area \\( A \\) of an equilateral triangle with circumradius \\( R \\) is given by the formula:\n\\[ A = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{4}R^2 \\]\nSubstituting \\( R = 7 \\):\n\\[ A = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times 7^2 = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times 49 \\]\n\nLet's compute this step by step:\n1. Calculate \\( 49 \\):\n\\[ 49 = 7^2 \\]\n\n2. Substitute \\( R = 7 \\) into the area formula:\n\\[ A = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\times 49 \\]\n\n3. Simplify the expression:\n\\[ A = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3} \\times 49}{4} \\]\n\\[ A = \\frac{3 \\times 49 \\sqrt{3}}{4} \\]\n\\[ A = \\frac{147\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\]\n\nFinally, we can express the area in a more readable format:\n\\[ A = \\boxed{\\frac{147\\sqrt{3}}{4}} \\]",
